                <pb facs="0251" n="218" xml:id="pg218"/>

                <head>156. Telegram From the Station in the Congo to the Central Intelligence
                        Agency<anchor n="1" xml:id="d156-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Leopoldville</placeName>,
                            <date calendar="gregorian" when="1963-11-06">November 6,
                        1963</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>8842 (In 55582). Ref Leop 8828 (In 53889);<anchor n="2" xml:id="d156-f2"/> Leop
                    8841 (In 55524).<anchor n="3" xml:id="d156-f3"/></p>
                <p>1. As noted in [Embassy] traffic cited refs, [Identity 1] has emerged from the events of last two weeks,
                    despite his well known shortcomings, as the one figure who by his relative
                    moderation and patience is able to set a limit to impulses of his colleagues in
                    direction ruthless authoritarian regime. Believe [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >cryptonym not declassified</hi>], despite their real fear of revolutionary
                    potential of unions, have recognized need consider international ramifications,
                    and particularly effect on PBPRIME public
                    opinion of methods they use. Thus we now again, as after so many previous
                    crises, emerge with only one logical contender, and [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >cryptonym not declassified</hi>] have been taught that there is some limit
                    to ODYOKE patience.</p>
                <p>2. However, it is clear that Station relations with [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >cryptonym not declassified</hi>] would have been strained even without
                    impact of [Embassy] reaction to recent actions of triumvirate in view of what
                    they consider inadequacy our contribution to their political party effort.</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion of funding.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d156-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d156-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Central Intelligence Agency Files, Job 78–00435R, DDO/ISS Files, Box 1,
                    Folder 2, [<hi rendition="#italic">cryptonym not declassified</hi>] Support.
                    Secret; Rybat; [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >cryptonym not declassified</hi>]. Received at 1305Z.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d156-f2" n="2" xml:id="d156-f2-content">
                <p>[<hi rendition="#italic">cryptonym not declassified</hi>] [Ambassador Gullion] indicated his belief that KUBARK [<hi rendition="#italic">less than 1
                        line not declassified</hi>] should increase its contribution to [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">cryptonym not declassified</hi>]. [Footnote in the
                    original.] Regarding this telegram, see <ref target="#d154-f4">footnote 4 to
                        Document 154</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d156-f3" n="3" xml:id="d156-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram 8841 from Leopoldville to CIA, November
                    6, stated that the Station believed that what had chiefly upset the [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">text not declassified</hi>] was the small size of the
                    proposed electoral contributions compared to their hopes, in spite of the fact
                    that they previously had been warned of the U.S. intention to introduce
                    economies and stricter accounting. (Central Intelligence Agency Files, Job
                    78–00435R, DDO/ISS Files, Box 2, Folder 7, [<hi rendition="#italic">cryptonym
                        not declassified</hi>] Operations)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
